-- #1111 A-2.6: the guard on a DELIBERATE OMISSION, not on a behaviour change.
--
-- A `UseGroup` member naming ONLY a TYPE (`hja.{HA}` — no `(..)`, no ctor member)
-- binds no constructor, so `importedCtorTypeDecls` contributes NO overlay for it.
-- That omission is load-bearing: the overlay is registered LAST-WINS in source order,
-- so if a type-only member did contribute, `hja`'s decl would be registered after
-- `hjz`'s and `HA`'s `Hit` would HIJACK the `Hit` this file actually imports, one
-- clause later, from `hjz`.  `Hit "s"` would then be `Hit : Int -> HA` and the
-- program would be rejected.
--
-- Expected output, derived by hand: `s`.  `Hit` is imported by name from `hjz` only,
-- so `Hit "s" : HZ` and `unHZ v` is `"s"`.
--
-- ⚠️ HONEST SCOPE: this fixture is GREEN on pristine `main` too (verified first-hand
-- on a binary built from 40756bea: `check` exit 0, `run` prints `s`) — main has no
-- overlay for a type-only member either, so it cannot hijack.  It therefore pins a
-- PRESERVED property, not a repaired one.  It is here because A-2.6 is the change
-- that makes the omission a CHOICE rather than an absence, and the obvious
-- "simplification" of the new rule — let any member contribute its owning decl — is
-- exactly what this file catches.  Delete the `variantNamed`/`ctors` restriction in
-- `memberOverlayDecl` and this goes red; the two drain fixtures do not.
import hja.{HA}
import hjz.{HZ, Hit, unHZ}

v : HZ
v = Hit "s"

main : <IO> Unit
main = putStrLn (unHZ v)
